# The contents of a book

A book's contents are its parts and their chapters, in the order a reader takes them. Nobody writes them down. A list kept by hand drifts from the book it describes: a part with no loom behind it, a chapter listed in the wrong place, a number that skips. A chapter already declares where it belongs, though. Its frontmatter names its part and its position, as `Part VIII, Chapter 11: The contents of a book` does, so Loom reads that placement back out of the corpus whenever something needs the contents. The weave is the main reader: it turns the contents into the woven book's navigation.

Not every chapter has a number. A part declared with a bare `Part: Work in progress` and chapters declared `Chapter: <title>` is numberless — a group the book carries but does not sequence, ordered by whoever reads it rather than by position. A numberless chapter may also carry a `Status` and a `Priority`, the metadata a roadmap reads. So the same pass that orders the numbered book also gathers the numberless work beside it, and a presentation renders one as a contents list and the other as a board.

## The shape of the contents

A `TableOfContents` is the corpus's own structure: an ordered list of parts, the back matter, and the loose chapters that belong to no part. A `TocPart` carries its `number` — empty when the part is numberless — its `name`, and its `chapters` in order. A `TocChapter` carries its own `number` (again empty when numberless), its `title`, the optional `status` and `priority` a board reads, the optional `language` its frontmatter names, which a menu can mark the chapter by, and the `path` of the loom it came from, so a presentation resolves it to a page. Everything in it is read from frontmatter.

The back matter follows the parts and lists the book's discussions. A `TocBackMatter` carries the discussions in order. A `TocDiscussion` carries its `place`, its own `number` (empty when it has none), its `title`, and the `path` of its loom. The place names the discussion's part: `Part I` when the part has a number, and the part's name when it has none.

=>

export const TocChapterSchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  status: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  priority: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  language: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  path: Schema.String,
})
export type TocChapter = typeof TocChapterSchema.Type

export const TocPartSchema = Schema.Struct({
  number: Schema.String,
  name: Schema.String,
  chapters: Schema.Array(TocChapterSchema),
})
export type TocPart = typeof TocPartSchema.Type

export const TocDiscussionSchema = Schema.Struct({
  place: Schema.String,
  number: Schema.String,
  title: Schema.String,
  path: Schema.String,
})
export type TocDiscussion = typeof TocDiscussionSchema.Type

export const TocBackMatterSchema = Schema.Struct({
  discussions: Schema.Array(TocDiscussionSchema),
})
export type TocBackMatter = typeof TocBackMatterSchema.Type

export const TableOfContentsSchema = Schema.Struct({
  parts: Schema.Array(TocPartSchema),
  backMatter: TocBackMatterSchema,
  loose: Schema.Array(TocChapterSchema),
})
export type TableOfContents = typeof TableOfContentsSchema.Type

## The builder and its spine

`buildContents` reads the contents out of a corpus, and the weave calls it directly. It is a pure function, since reading the frontmatter waits on nothing.

It gathers every chapter that declares a place, and folds each part into a `TocPart`: its number and name from the part's declaration, and its chapters from the ones that name it, in order. The parts come out numbered-first, so the book reads Part I to Part XI and then its numberless parts, however the corpus happened to load. A chapter that names no part at all is loose, gathered on its own. The discussions gather into the back matter, ordered by their parts.

=>

export const buildContents = (corpus: LoomCorpusAst): TableOfContents => {
  const modules = Array.fromIterable(corpus.modules.values())
  const chapters = Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, chapterDeclOf))
  const parts = pipe(
    dedupeParts(Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, partDeclOf))),
    Array.sort(byPart),
    Array.map((decl) =>
      TocPartSchema.make({
        number: decl.number,
        name: decl.name,
        chapters: chaptersUnder(chapters, identityOf(decl)),
      }),
    ),
  )
  const loose = pipe(
    Array.filter(chapters, (chapter) => chapter.partId === undefined),
    Array.map(toTocChapter),
  )
  const backMatter = TocBackMatterSchema.make({
    discussions: pipe(
      Array.getSomes(Array.map(modules, discussionDeclOf)),
      Array.sort(byDiscussion),
      Array.map(toTocDiscussion),
    ),
  })
  return TableOfContentsSchema.make({ parts, backMatter, loose })
}

## A chapter's place in the corpus

A part is declared by any loom that names it: a numbered part in an opener's `Part R: name` line, a numberless part in a bare `Part: name`. `partDeclOf` reads that declaration — the number, empty when there is none, and the name. A chapter is any loom with a `title` that is no discussion, and `chapterDeclOf` reads it: the part it belongs to, its own number, its title, its status and priority, and its path. A chapter names its part by number when the part is numbered and by name when it is numberless, so `partId` is one or the other — and absent when the loom declares no part, which makes the chapter loose.

=>

type PartDecl = { readonly number: string; readonly name: string }

type Chapter = {
  readonly partId: string | undefined
  readonly number: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly status: string | undefined
  readonly priority: string | undefined
  readonly language: string | undefined
  readonly path: string
}

const partDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<PartDecl> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.flatMap((frontmatter) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.partName), (partName) => ({
        number: frontmatter.part?.value ?? '',
        name: partName.value,
      })),
    ),
  )

const chapterDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Chapter> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.filter((frontmatter) => frontmatter.discussion === undefined),
    Option.flatMap((frontmatter) =>
      Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.title), (title) => ({
        partId: frontmatter.part?.value ?? frontmatter.partName?.value,
        number: frontmatter.chapter?.value ?? '',
        title: title.value,
        status: frontmatter.status?.value,
        priority: frontmatter.priority?.value,
        language: frontmatter.language?.value,
        path: module.path,
      })),
    ),
  )

const toTocChapter = (chapter: Chapter): TocChapter =>
  TocChapterSchema.make({
    number: chapter.number,
    title: chapter.title,
    status: chapter.status,
    priority: chapter.priority,
    language: chapter.language,
    path: chapter.path,
  })

## Ordering parts and chapters

A part's identity is its number when it has one and its name when it does not, so `identityOf` reads whichever the part carries and a chapter finds its part by the same key. `dedupeParts` keeps one declaration per identity, since every loom in a numberless part repeats the part's name.

Numbered parts sort by their Roman numeral, numberless parts fall after them by name, and `byPart` orders both at once. `romanValue` reads a Roman numeral as an integer: each letter is worth its unit, less that unit when a larger one follows it, so `IX` is nine and `XII` is twelve. `byNumber` orders a part's chapters the same way: numbered ones by their Arabic number, then numberless ones by title. `chaptersUnder` gathers the chapters that name one part and sorts them.

=>

const romanUnits: Record<string, number> = {
  I: 1,
  V: 5,
  X: 10,
  L: 50,
  C: 100,
  D: 500,
  M: 1000,
}

const romanValue = (roman: string): number => {
  const units = Array.map(roman.toUpperCase().split(''), (letter) => romanUnits[letter] ?? 0)
  return pipe(
    units,
    Array.map((unit, index) => (unit < (units[index + 1] ?? 0) ? -unit : unit)),
    Array.reduce(0, (sum, unit) => sum + unit),
  )
}

const identityOf = (part: PartDecl): string => (part.number === '' ? part.name : part.number)

const dedupeParts = (decls: ReadonlyArray<PartDecl>): ReadonlyArray<PartDecl> =>
  Array.reduce(decls, [] as ReadonlyArray<PartDecl>, (kept, decl) =>
    Array.some(kept, (seen) => identityOf(seen) === identityOf(decl)) ? kept : [...kept, decl],
  )

const byPart: Order.Order<PartDecl> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (part: PartDecl) => (part.number === '' ? 1 : 0)),
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (part: PartDecl) =>
    part.number === '' ? 0 : romanValue(part.number),
  ),
  Order.mapInput(Order.String, (part: PartDecl) => part.name),
])

type Numbered = { readonly number: string; readonly title: string }

const byNumber: Order.Order<Numbered> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (entry: Numbered) => (entry.number === '' ? 1 : 0)),
  Order.mapInput(Order.Number, (entry: Numbered) =>
    entry.number === '' ? 0 : Number(entry.number),
  ),
  Order.mapInput(Order.String, (entry: Numbered) => entry.title),
])

const chaptersUnder = (
  chapters: ReadonlyArray<Chapter>,
  identity: string,
): ReadonlyArray<TocChapter> =>
  pipe(
    Array.filter(chapters, (chapter) => chapter.partId === identity),
    Array.sort(byNumber),
    Array.map(toTocChapter),
  )

## The discussions at the back

A discussion stands in its part beside the chapters, but the contents list it apart from them, at the back of the book under a `Back matter` heading. `discussionDeclOf` reads a discussion the way `chapterDeclOf` reads a chapter: its part, its own number, its title, and its path. A discussion that names no part has no place to list it by, so it stays out of the back matter, as a loose chapter stays out of the parts.

`byDiscussion` orders the discussions by their parts first, as `byPart` orders the parts, and then by their own numbers, as `byNumber` orders chapters. `toTocDiscussion` writes the discussion's part as its place.

=>

type Discussion = {
  readonly part: PartDecl
  readonly number: string
  readonly title: string
  readonly path: string
}

export const backMatterHeading = 'Back matter'

const discussionDeclOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<Discussion> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter),
    Option.flatMap((frontmatter) =>
      pipe(
        Option.all({
          discussion: Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.discussion),
          title: Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.title),
          part: Option.fromNullishOr(frontmatter.part ?? frontmatter.partName),
        }),
        Option.map(({ discussion, title }) => ({
          part: { number: frontmatter.part?.value ?? '', name: frontmatter.partName?.value ?? '' },
          number: discussion.value,
          title: title.value,
          path: module.path,
        })),
      ),
    ),
  )

const byDiscussion: Order.Order<Discussion> = Order.combineAll([
  Order.mapInput(byPart, (discussion: Discussion) => discussion.part),
  byNumber,
])

const toTocDiscussion = (discussion: Discussion): TocDiscussion =>
  TocDiscussionSchema.make({
    place: discussion.part.number === '' ? discussion.part.name : `Part ${discussion.part.number}`,
    number: discussion.number,
    title: discussion.title,
    path: discussion.path,
  })

## What the builder draws on

The builder reads the Loom AST and writes the contents model, so it draws from both. From `effect` come the array and option combinators, `Order` for the sort, `pipe` for the spine, and `Schema` for the model. From the Loom side come the corpus and the module a chapter is.

=>

import { Array, Option, Order, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import { type LoomCorpusAst, type LoomModule } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'

